// ============================================================================
== 5.1 The Frame: Dusk to Dawn

The first chapter is called *Dusk* and the last is called *…Dawn*. Neither title names
the time of the action — the assembly in Chapter 1 is a morning assembly — and that is the
point. The titles are moods, and they enclose the book.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch05_frame.svg",
  "Dusk, the long night, and Dawn. The two titles are the frame, and the ellipsis before Dawn is part of the design: the day is not quite here.", width: 100%)

#concept-box("What the frame does")[
  A frame makes the ending inevitable and the middle a test. Because Chapter 1 is called *Dusk*, the reader knows before the first sentence that something is going down; because Chapter 12 is called *…Dawn*, the reader knows that whatever happens in between, the book is travelling towards light.

  The novel uses the device to hold a sad story and a hopeful ending in the same cover without pretending the sadness was not real.
]

// ============================================================================
== 5.2 The Narrator Beside Bepo

The novel is written in the third person, and the third person is limited to Bepo. The
reader is told what he thinks, remembers and fears, and is told nothing that he does not
know.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch05_narrator.svg",
  "Third person, close to Bepo: what the narrator shows, what it withholds, and why the difference matters.", width: 100%)

#rule-box("The narrator, stated three ways")[
  + *The person:* third person — *he*, *Bepo*, never *I*.
  + *The limit:* limited — the reader stays inside Bepo's knowledge and cannot see what Seri or Mrs Gloss thinks in private.
  + *The effect:* every judgement in the book arrives with the weight of fact, and the reader has to notice when Bepo is rationalising. The scene in which he delays his passport for two years is comic because we are inside the excuse as he makes it.
]

#trap-box("Third person does not mean distant")[
  A candidate who reads *third person* and concludes that the novel is detached has confused grammar with temperature. This narrator is close enough to report the exact arithmetic in Bepo's head — ₦400,000 against £3,600 — and that arithmetic is the plot.
]

// ============================================================================
== 5.3 Symbol and Motif

Four objects carry more than their weight in this novel, and each one appears at a moment
the plot needs it.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch05_symbols.svg",
  "The four symbols, where each one appears, and what it carries. A symbol question asks what the object does, not what it is.", width: 100%)

#concept-box("The snake in the roof, unpacked")[
  Chief Mrs Solape Bayo's proverb — *it is like hanging a snake in the roof and going to bed* — is the novel's tightest image. A snake in the roof is a danger you have put there yourself, and you sleep under it every night. In her mouth it means the staff: feed them, lend them money, and one day they will bite.

  The proverb is also the novel's judgement on her. What she calls a snake, the reader can see is a profession becoming independent. The style does the work of an argument in one sentence, which is what proverbs are for.
]

#shortcut-box("How to write a symbol answer in two sentences")[
  + *Sentence one: what it is and where it appears.* “The snake in the roof is Chief Bayo's proverb for the staff cooperative in Chapter 5.”
  + *Sentence two: what it does.* “It turns a threat she has created into a threat she merely fears, and it shows the board's attitude to its own teachers.”

  Two sentences, a scene and a meaning. Anything shorter is a label.
]

// ============================================================================
== 5.4 Language: Proverbs, Humour, Irony and Nigerian English

The style is plain on the surface and patterned underneath. Four features do most of the
work.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch05_language.svg",
  "Four features of the style, with an example in view and the reason the novel uses each one.", width: 100%)

#rule-box("The four features, with their work")[
  + *Proverbs* compress an argument into an image and put it in the mouth of the person who feels it. *The carver leaves, the carvings remain* is the whole of Chapter 11.
  + *Humour* lets the novel say hard things without preaching. Audu's jokes arrive at the exact moment the book would otherwise become a sermon.
  + *Irony* puts the argument inside the character. A headmaster condemning japa while packing for London is the novel's central irony, and it is never stated.
  + *Nigerian English* locates the story: *japa*, *owambe*, *iru*, *egusi*, the idiom *other things being equal*, the shout of *Noooo!* The language is the setting heard rather than described.
]

#trap-box("Do not call the style simple")[
  The sentences are short and the vocabulary is ordinary, and a candidate often writes *the language is simple* as if that were the end of the analysis. The plainness is a choice: it keeps a sad story readable, it lets characters speak like people, and it makes the few lyrical moments — the waterfalls, the Point of No Return — land. *Accessible* is the word; *simple* is a judgement the paper will not reward.
]

// ============================================================================
== 5.5 Answering a Style Question

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch05_style_questions.svg",
  "The five style questions the section asks, what each one wants, and the move that answers it.", width: 100%)

#rule-box("Device plus purpose, every time")[
  + *Name* the device: proverb, metaphor, irony, humour, symbol, repetition, code-mixing.
  + *Place* it: chapter and speaker.
  + *Say what it does*: what the reader understands because of it that they would not otherwise understand.
  + *Stop.* Two sentences is enough. A third sentence usually repeats the second.
]

#worked-example(
  1,
  "The narrative person",
  [The novel is narrated in the

    A. first person by Bepo \
    B. third person, limited to Bepo \
    C. third person by an omniscient narrator \
    D. first person by Mrs Gloss],
  [
    *Step 1 — the pronoun.* The book says *he*, *Bepo*, *the principal* — never *I*. That rules out A and D immediately.

    *Step 2 — the limit.* The narrator knows Bepo's thoughts and not other people's. An omniscient narrator would know what Seri thinks in London; this one does not, because Bepo does not.

    *Step 3 — the effect.* The limit is why the novel feels intimate and why its judgements can be wrong. C is the plausible option for a candidate who stops at *third person*.

    *Check.* Ask whose knowledge the narrator shares. The answer is *B*.
  ],
  tier: "FOUNDATION",
)

#worked-example(
  2,
  "The title of Chapter 1",
  [The title of Chapter 1, *Dusk*, suggests

    A. the time of the morning assembly \
    B. a mood of failing light and approaching darkness \
    C. the end of the school term \
    D. Bepo's retirement],
  [
    *Step 1 — the literal reading.* The assembly happens in the morning, so A is not merely wrong but impossible — which is the point of the title.

    *Step 2 — the figurative reading.* Dusk is the light going, the day failing, the moment before night. A man who breaks down in front of his school is standing in it.

    *Step 3 — the pair.* The last chapter is *…Dawn*. The two titles together are the structure, and a question on one is usually a question on both.

    *Check.* When a chapter title contradicts the time of day, the title is doing figurative work. The answer is *B*.
  ],
  tier: "FOUNDATION",
)

#worked-example(
  3,
  "The snake in the roof",
  [Chief Mrs Solape Bayo's remark that the staff are like a snake hung in the roof is an example of

    A. a simile used to describe the school building \
    B. a proverb used as a metaphor for a danger the board has housed itself \
    C. hyperbole used to frighten the teachers \
    D. an idiom meaning that the school is unsafe],
  [
    *Step 1 — the form.* It is a proverb — a fixed saying offered as wisdom — used metaphorically. The snake is not literal.

    *Step 2 — the meaning.* A danger you have put in your own roof and sleep under: the staff, fed and lent money by the school, who might one day leave and compete.

    *Step 3 — the near misses.* A confuses it with a simile and with the building; C names a device the sentence does not use; D is close in feeling and wrong in detail, because the danger in the proverb is *inside*, not merely present.

    *Check.* Device, then purpose. The purpose here is the board's fear of its own teachers. The answer is *B*.
  ],
  tier: "UTME STANDARD",
)

#worked-example(
  4,
  "The shout in the drama",
  [Bepo's shout of *Noooo!* during the drama club's dance is significant because

    A. it shows that he dislikes the performance \
    B. the memory of Badagry breaks into the present and foreshadows his decision \
    C. it proves that he has lost his voice \
    D. it is the moment he decides to travel],
  [
    *Step 1 — the scene.* The dance is about the Badagry visit; the museum comes back to him; he shouts before he knows he has; then he covers it with a joke about the canoe.

    *Step 2 — the function.* The shout makes the inner argument visible in public. It is also the rehearsal for Chapter 12, when the same word is shouted in the dream on the plane.

    *Step 3 — the near misses.* A misreads the joke that follows; C confuses this with Chapter 1; D reverses the decision, which is made later and in the opposite direction.

    *Check.* Find the echo. The same word in Chapters 11 and 12 is a deliberate pattern. The answer is *B*.
  ],
  tier: "UTME STANDARD",
)

#worked-example(
  5,
  "What japa is doing in the prose",
  [The novel's use of the word *japa* is best described as

    A. a spelling error in the Nigerian edition \
    B. a local idiom that names the theme in the language of its characters \
    C. a foreign loan word used for decoration \
    D. a technical term from migration studies],
  [
    *Step 1 — the word.* *Japa* is Yoruba for to flee or escape, and in Nigerian English it has become the name for emigration.

    *Step 2 — the effect.* Using it puts the theme in the mouths of the people living it, rather than in the vocabulary of a report. It also dates and locates the novel precisely.

    *Step 3 — the near misses.* A and D are wrong about what the word is; C is wrong about why it is there — a loan word used for decoration would not carry the title of a chapter and the argument of the book.

    *Check.* Style questions reward the answer that connects the language to the meaning. The answer is *B*.
  ],
  tier: "JAMB CHALLENGE",
)

