== 5.6 Chapter 5 in the Exam Hall — a Two-Minute Set

#mcq(1, [The novel is narrated in the], [first person], [second person], [third person], [stream of consciousness])

#mcq(2, [The pair of chapter titles that frames the novel is], [Dusk and Dawn], [Ritualists and Dawn], [Dusk and Passport Pains], [The Enticement and Point of No Return])

#mcq(3, [The proverb about hanging a snake in the roof is spoken by], [Mrs Ibidun Gloss], [Chief Mrs Solape Bayo], [Mr Audu], [Pastor Ope Wande])

#mcq(4, [The phrase *other things being equal*, repeated by Bepo, suggests], [his training as an economist], [a habit of cautious, conditional speech], [that he is unsure of English], [that he is quoting the MD])

#answer-key-grid(("C", "A", "B", "B"))

#answer-notes((
  ("C", [Third person, limited to Bepo. A and D are the two ways a candidate who notices the closeness of the thoughts gets the person wrong.]),
  ("A", [Dusk and …Dawn. B, C and D all pair real chapter titles, which is what makes them tempting — but only the first and the last form the frame.]),
  ("B", [The board chairman, Chief Mrs Solape Bayo. A is her daughter and runs the school, C is the wag, and D is the pastor — all present in Chapter 5's world, none of them the speaker.]),
  ("B", [It is a verbal tic that hedges every claim — the speech of a man who has learned to argue carefully. A and C read it as a qualification or a fault, and D invents a source.]),
))

